## Two things I changed beyond the literal ask
Both because Fix 1 made existing text false — flagging them so you can revert if you disagree:

1. **The build-order remark** in all 9 specs said *"Stage 4, the Belgium Data Dictionary, does not exist yet. No DD cross-check step was performed and none is claimed."* Sitting next to 318 DD-cited cells, that's self-contradictory. Rewritten per file to state the DD now exists at v1.1, the cross-check is recorded in `DD-CROSSCHECK-TABLE.md`, and why the remaining N zones stay NOT FOUND. The WTC/`Mercans Code` caveat is preserved verbatim — I did not touch `BE-WTC-001-v1.0` references, as that's your separate task.
2. **A v1.2 changelog row** added to each spec (row 15, dated 2026-08-16) recording both fixes with per-file counts — a 318-cell content change with no version record is what the QA gate flags.

Note the v1.2 changelog text deliberately contains the string `BE-DD-001-v1.0` ("21 references to BE-DD-001-v1.0 updated to…"), since a changelog has to name what it changed from. That's the only remaining v1.0 occurrence in each file — I confirmed cell-by-cell that no *live* pointer is still at v1.0.

Structural integrity checked: no charts, drawings, data validations or conditional formatting exist in these workbooks, so the openpyxl round-trip is lossless. I tested `insert_rows` first and found it shifts values but leaves merged ranges behind — it would have misaligned the whole REMARKS block by one row — so I wrote into the already-empty row 15 instead and asserted merge ranges intact afterward.
